<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Æthelred of Mercia</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. 675–704</span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Æthelred was king of Mercia from 675 to 704, a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/penda-of-mercia">Penda</a>. In 679 he defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ecgfrith-of-northumbria">Ecgfrith of Northumbria</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-trent">Battle of the Trent</a>, recovering the province of Lindsey for Mercia permanently. A patron of the church, he abdicated in 704 to become a monk and later abbot of Bardney in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lincolnshire">Lincolnshire</a>, leaving the throne to his nephew Coenred.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/aethelred-of-mercia.jpg" alt="Æthelred of Mercia" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Wolfgang Sauber · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:St_John%27s_Church,_Chester_-_Westfenster_2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Æthelred was the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/penda-of-mercia">Penda of Mercia</a>. His likely mother was Penda's queen, Cynewise, named by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bede">Bede</a>, though this is not certain. His date of birth is unknown, and nothing is known of his childhood. He had brothers, Wulfhere and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peada">Peada</a>, and sisters, Cyneburh and Cyneswith. Mercia in his time occupied the English midlands, with Northumbria to the north, East Anglia to the east and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wessex">Wessex</a> to the south. By the early 670s Wulfhere had become the most powerful king in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, but in 674 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ecgfrith-of-northumbria">Ecgfrith of Northumbria</a> defeated him, took Lindsey and imposed tribute. Wulfhere died in 675, possibly of disease, and Æthelred became king.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>In 676 his armies ravaged Kent and destroyed Rochester, seat of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> of West Kent; the reason is not recorded. The bishop Putta retired and his successor Cwichhelm gave up the see because of its poverty. In 679 Æthelred defeated his brother-in-law Ecgfrith at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-trent">Battle of the Trent</a>. Ecgfrith's young brother Ælfwine was killed, but Archbishop Theodore arranged a peace with the customary compensation. Æthelred took Lindsey, and it stayed Mercian until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Viking</a> invasion of the 9th century. The battle effectively ended Northumbrian involvement south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/humber">Humber</a>, though he could not restore the dominance of his predecessors in the south.</p><p>Æthelred was known as a pious king, more famed for piety than skill in war, and granted lands to the church, including at Tetbury, Long Newnton and Somerford Keynes. During his reign Archbishop Theodore divided the great Mercian see into five dioceses. When Bishop <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wilfrid">Wilfrid</a> of York was driven into exile in 692, Æthelred made him bishop of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-angles">Middle Angles</a> and defended him at the Council of Austerfield in about 702. His wife Osthryth, daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oswiu">Oswiu of Northumbria</a>, was murdered in 697 by Mercian chieftains, according to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bede">Bede</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 704 Æthelred abdicated, becoming a monk and abbot at Bardney, which he and Osthryth had founded, and left the throne to his nephew Coenred. He still gave advice afterwards: Stephen of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ripon">Ripon</a> records him urging Coenred to make peace with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wilfrid">Wilfrid</a>. The date of his death is not recorded, but he was buried at Bardney, and he and Osthryth were later revered there as saints. His son Ceolred became king in 709 after Coenred left for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Æthelred of Mercia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His son Ecgfrith extended rule over the Picts and established a bishopric at Abercorn on the Forth, but lost Lindsey after defeat by <b>Æthelred of Mercia</b> at the Battle of the Trent in 679.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-northumbria">Kingdom of Northumbria · Military</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in Bardney Abbey, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=53.22,-0.33&amp;year=689" aria-label="Bardney Abbey on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4543 332 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Æthelred of Mercia"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4993 602h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4993 602h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=53.22,-0.33&amp;year=689">What was here in 689 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
